S=[
 (1,"Bauhaus","Art and design school","Gropius, Kandinsky, Moholy-Nagy, Bayer","https://artsandculture.google.com/story/OwURAJwcVUkTow",
  "Form follows function, and the smallest visual alphabet is the triangle, square and circle in the three primaries. Kandinsky's expected answer: yellow triangle, red square, blue circle. Bayer set text in lowercase only, to reduce the alphabet to its essential geometry.",
  "Wasel already marks tiers with a circle, a diamond and a triangle, so the tiers become the Bauhaus forms: a red square for high risk, a yellow triangle for medium, a blue circle for low. The page is three bands, one per form, on an asymmetric layout with a diagonal bar.",
  "You read by form and colour first, then the lowercase names inside each band.",
  "Three primary forms stacked as an abstract composition.",
  "The form-colour rule is a 1920s claim that later researchers found was largely learned, not universal. Colour-blind users depend on the shapes, which is why the shape stays.","S"),
 (2,"Constructivism","Art and design movement","Rodchenko, Lissitzky","https://internationalposter.com/style-primers/constructivist-posters/",
  "Art as an instrument for social purpose, not decoration. Text is a structural element and strong diagonals imply motion and urgency. Posters used strident slogans and high-contrast composition to be understood instantly by anyone.",
  "The product speaks in commands. The page is four slanted bars, each an imperative: CONFIRM the address, PREPAY first, CHECK quickly, SHIP now, with the names under it and a count set huge. There is no list of orders at all, only what to do.",
  "You read the command, then act on the names inside it.",
  "A red triangle and a black circle cut by a diagonal bar, after Lissitzky's wedge and circle.",
  "Imperatives can feel like shouting at a customer-service worker, and slanted text is slower to read in bulk. It suits a short list far better than a long one.","S"),
 (3,"Wabi-sabi and kintsugi","Japanese philosophy","Imperfection, impermanence, incompleteness","https://rossettiart.com/blogs/news/wabi-sabi-understanding-the-3-key-principles",
  "Nothing lasts, nothing is finished, nothing is perfect. Kintsugi mends broken pottery with gold and does not hide the fracture: breakage and repair are part of the object's history.",
  "Orders are vessels on a shelf. The longer one waits, the more it weathers; high-risk ones carry visible cracks, one per reason. When the customer's reply resolves a reason, the crack is filled with gold and stays visible, so the history of the order is the surface of the order.",
  "You glance at the shelf to see what is weathered and what is whole.",
  "An arch with one gold repair line through it.",
  "Time and repair are shown as texture, which is beautiful and imprecise. Exact waiting time and amounts must still be one tap away, and decorative cracks must never be mistaken for errors.","M"),
 (4,"Saul Bass","Graphic design and film titles","Saul Bass","https://blog.designcrowd.com/article/2175/graphic-designer-spotlight-saul-bass",
  "The problem defines the solution. A title sequence is the first act of the film and sets its tone; find a metaphor, not just the prettiest frame. Distil a whole story into a simple, angular graphic landscape.",
  "Every order is a three-frame strip of film: what they said, how it will end (a cut-paper shape with the score), and what you do (an arrow and a verb). Strips stack like a reel. The unit of the product is the story of one order.",
  "You scan the strips like contact sheets, and the arrow frame is the decision.",
  "A black angular arch with an orange arrow cutting into it, in cut-paper style.",
  "Three frames per order are heavy for 50 orders; the metaphor must be chosen well for each one, and a wrong metaphor misleads.","M"),
 (5,"Metro, Windows Phone","Interface design language","Microsoft Design Language 2","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metro_(design_language)",
  "Four principles: typography, motion, content not chrome, and honesty. Based on classic Swiss graphic design and influenced by public transport signs; flat colour live tiles replace icons.",
  "A horizontal panorama of sections (orders, messages, results) whose huge light titles run off the screen edge, hinting at the next section. Each section is a hub of flat live tiles: a big '4 need you', a tile that rotates through the customer's quote. Content is the interface; there are no borders.",
  "You swipe sideways between sections and tap a tile, which suits a thumb.",
  "A flat orange tile with a white arch outline.",
  "Horizontal panoramas hide that more exists, and live tiles can be noise. Microsoft itself replaced Metro with Fluent. It suits a phone far better than a desk.","M"),
 (6,"Newsroom: the inverted pyramid","Journalistic practice","Wire-service news writing","https://concepts.dsebastien.net/concept/inverted-pyramid/",
  "Put the most critical facts first and the rest in descending order of importance, judged by relative news value. It respects reader time, supports skimming and lets an editor cut from the bottom.",
  "A front page ranked by news value. The riskiest order is the lead story with a headline and a lede (who, what, why); the next are briefs; the low-risk ones are one line at the bottom. You act as editor: Run it (send the question), Hold, or Spike (cancel).",
  "You read the lead, run or spike it, and work down the page.",
  "An inverted pyramid with a door notch.",
  "Headlines must be generated and verified, and a wrong lede is worse than a wrong row. A front page is great for ten orders and silly for a hundred.","M"),
 (7,"Field triage (START)","Emergency medicine system","Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment","https://chemm.hhs.gov/startalgotext.htm",
  "Greatest good for the greatest number. Categorise, do not diagnose: three quick checks in under a minute and a colour tag (red immediate, yellow delayed, green minor, black expectant) that decides who gets resources first.",
  "The screen is a field with four coloured zones and orders hung as triage tags. Each tag has three checks (address, value, history) and a tear-off strip with the action. Sorting is by rule, not by browsing: you work the red zone first.",
  "You clear zones in order of colour and tear off the action strip.",
  "A triage tag with a red band, a hole and a door.",
  "Triage is built for scarcity and speed; Wasel's volumes are small, so it can feel dramatic. The black zone (do not treat) has no honest equivalent here.","M"),
 (8,"Airport signage (Frutiger and Aicher)","Wayfinding design","Adrian Frutiger, Otl Aicher","https://madegooddesigns.com/frutiger-font/",
  "Signs must be legible at extreme angles, in peripheral vision, from moving walkways. Frutiger blurred letterforms to test them; a character had to have the same clarity as an arrow.",
  "A departures board for orders: each row is a destination (the next action), an order code, a pictogram and a large arrow: confirm, prepay, quick check, ship. Direction is the main information; the seller follows arrows, not paragraphs.",
  "You glance, pick an arrow, and go.",
  "A dark sign panel with a yellow arrow pointing up through a door.",
  "Arrows say where to go but not why; the reasons live one tap deeper. A board needs a legend, and a long board becomes wallpaper.","S"),
 (9,"Pacioli's ledger","Accounting tradition","Luca Pacioli, 1494","https://chapman.edu/about/our-home/busts-collection/pacioli.aspx",
  "Every debit has a twin credit and they always go together; the sum of debits equals the sum of credits. The system lets a merchant reconcile every transaction, find errors and settle disputes.",
  "Each order is a T-account. The left side lists what raises the risk (debits), the right what lowers it (credits), and the balance is the score. A journal records each entry in order. A customer reply is a new credit entry that moves the balance, 87 to 16, and the book still balances.",
  "You audit an order like a bookkeeper: read the entries, trust the balance.",
  "A T with a double rule beneath it, the accountant's closing line.",
  "The most transparent direction and the most clerical. It suits the person who wants to check the maths and bores everyone else.","M"),
 (10,"Cybersyn and management by exception","Cybernetic management room","Stafford Beer, Gui Bonsiepe","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Cybersyn",
  "Four levels of control with algedonic feedback: if one level does not fix a problem in time, the next level up is told. Decision-making is devolved so the room only speaks when something leaves its limits.",
  "A bright, almost empty room. A single ring says 7 of 9 orders are within limits, and only the two that were escalated to you appear, each showing what the assistant already tried. A footer shows the escalation chain: customer, assistant, you.",
  "You mostly do nothing; you take over only what has been escalated.",
  "A grey ring with one orange signal at its top.",
  "Only works if the limits are right. If Wasel's thresholds are wrong, the room is calm while orders fail, which is the worst failure. It needs the prototype's accuracy limits to be spelled out first.","L"),
 (11,"Macintosh and direct manipulation","Interface design","Susan Kare, Bill Atkinson, Apple","https://99percentinvisible.org/article/designed-with-kare-influential-macintosh-graphics-of-early-apple-computers/",
  "A computer anyone could use without a manual, friendly rather than technical. Icons are economy of expression: more like road signs than illustrations. The desktop metaphor lets you act on things directly.",
  "A desktop. Orders are document icons in a window; the decisions are folders you drag them onto (ask for the address, ask for prepayment, ship) and a cancel bin. Deciding is a physical act, not a button inside a form.",
  "You drag an order onto the action. Double-click to read it.",
  "A pixel-drawn door icon in one-bit style.",
  "It is the most nostalgic and the most likely to be a costume. Dragging is slow on a phone and needs a tap alternative. The one-bit look must not become the whole identity.","M"),
 (12,"Hashiya: the commentary page","Arabic manuscript tradition","Marginal glosses and commentary","https://www.saw-leipzig.de/en/research/projects/bibliotheca-arabica/publications/stefanie-brinkmann-ed-marginal-matters-explorations-into-commenting-and-glossing-techniques-in-arabic-manuscript-cultures",
  "For centuries readers wrote in the margins and between lines, explaining, criticising and supplementing the main text. The margin is a productive space of interpretation, not a periphery.",
  "The customer's own message is the central text, set in a ruled frame in Arabic script and Arabizi. The reasons are glosses around it, each pointing at the exact phrase with the points it added, plus a gloss that translates Darija into plain words. The product is a close reading.",
  "You read the message, then the commentary tilted around it, then act.",
  "A ruled frame with a central panel and small diagonal glosses at its corners.",
  "It is the most native to this product's content and the least scannable: one order per page. It needs the margin to be real commentary, not decoration, and the translations to be verified.","M"),
 (13,"Hurufiyya and the calligram","Arab modernist art movement","Madiha Omar, Nja Mahdaoui","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurufiyya_movement",
  "Artists stripped letters down to their visual elements and used the gesture of the calligrapher within the language of modern art, building a culture-specific visual language. Mahdaoui, the 'choreographer of letters', is Tunisian.",
  "The order is a composition. The customer's words are set large and flowing along curves; the phrases that raised the risk are in heavy black ink and the rest in pale grey, so risk is literally ink density. The score is a round red seal in the corner, and a column of small seals indexes the orders.",
  "You read the composition for the heavy strokes, then stamp the decision.",
  "A red seal ring with a black calligraphic stroke through it.",
  "Beautiful and ambiguous. It needs an honest plain-text version for anyone who cannot read stylised Arabic, and the heavy words must exactly match the reasons.","L"),
 (14,"Imhof's terrain","Cartography","Eduard Imhof, Swiss topographic mapping","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrain_cartography",
  "Show the terrain as a three-dimensional surface with shaded relief and contour lines, oblique light from the upper left, and subtle colour that rises with elevation. Only simplicity provides a lasting impression.",
  "Risk becomes a landscape. One axis is how clear the address is, the other is order value; the height of the ground is the risk, drawn with contour lines every 20 points and a hypsometric tint. Orders are survey points with a spot height (Sami, 87 m). The summit is where a vague address meets a high value.",
  "You see the hills first, then walk to the nearest peak.",
  "Nested contour arches, the lines of a hill that are also a door.",
  "A map invents a continuous surface from nine points, which can look more certain than it is. It answers 'where is the danger' and not 'what do I do next'.","L"),
 (15,"The IKEA manual","Democratic design","Gillis Lundgren, flat-pack instructions","https://justinzhuang.com/posts/wordless-instructions/",
  "Instructions understood regardless of language, culture or experience, using line drawings. Every step is small enough that no single action overwhelms, even when the whole is complex.",
  "Each order is handled like an assembly manual: step 1 read what he wrote, step 2 the missing part (a full address, shown as the parts bag), step 3 send the question and wait. Wordless line drawings, one step highlighted, and a tick when a part arrives.",
  "You follow the steps in order, one highlighted at a time.",
  "A line-drawn house with a numbered blue circle in the doorway.",
  "Charming and slow. Fine for a new seller, tedious for an expert, and the drawings must be made for each kind of problem.","M"),
 (16,"Ranganathan's facets","Library science","S. R. Ranganathan, Colon Classification","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colon_classification",
  "Hierarchical schemes are too limiting, because an item can belong to more than one subject. Describe it by facets: personality, matter, energy, space and time. Search can move along multiple paths.",
  "Browsing by facets instead of one list: personality (new or returning customer), matter (the goods), energy (what to do), space (rural or urban), time (how long waiting). Every order carries a short class mark such as N:H:C:R:3h, and your current filters read as one chain of colon-joined chips.",
  "You narrow by ticking facets until the list is the work you want to do.",
  "A colon (two dots) beside a door arch.",
  "It is faceted search, which every ecommerce tool already has, with a distinctive notation. The notation is a cute signature but must not become a code sellers have to learn.","M"),
 (17,"The aviation checklist","Human factors","Boeing Model 299, Gawande","https://www.process.st/how-a-checklist-saved-boeing-from-bankruptcy-and-helped-win-world-war-ii/",
  "Complexity exceeds any one person's ability to get everything right under pressure, so skilled people overlook the obvious. A short checklist, small enough to fit on an index card, made the Flying Fortress flyable.",
  "Before shipping, each order has a checklist card: message read, address complete, value within limit, prior deliveries, phone given. Wasel fills in what it can; a pause line forbids handing over to the courier until the address is complete, and ABORT is a red button.",
  "You confirm the open items, and the button to ship stays locked until they are done.",
  "A boxed square with a heavy check mark.",
  "Checklists add friction by design. They work for the few items that matter and fail if there are too many; Wasel's list must stay short.","M"),
 (18,"Chiaroscuro","Painting technique","Caravaggio, Rembrandt","https://learnart.app/learn/chiaroscuro",
  "Light directs attention. A single dominant light source outside the frame and strong contrast guide the eye and build urgency, as in Caravaggio's The Calling of Saint Matthew.",
  "The room is dark and one light falls from the upper left. Hierarchy is brightness, not size or colour: a score of 89 sits in the light and a score of 9 is in half shadow, all rows the same size. The lit order opens beside the list with its risky phrases picked out in gold.",
  "You are drawn to the brightest row, and that is the one that needs you.",
  "A door arch lit on one half and dark on the other.",
  "Dim rows must still meet accessible contrast, which limits how dark the shadows can go. It is dramatic and could feel theatrical for a routine task.","S"),
 (19,"Zellige: the finished panel","Maghrebi craft","Maâlems of Fez","https://www.ziatile.com/blog/zellige-mosaics",
  "Thousands of hand-cut glazed tesserae assembled from a limited vocabulary of shapes into a pattern that comes right when finished. The craft is passed from master to apprentice.",
  "Today is one panel of nine octagonal tiles. A tile is bare biscuit until you decide the order, then it is glazed in its tier colour. The day is finished when no tile is bare. The reward for clearing the work is a completed pattern, not an empty inbox.",
  "You work the bare tiles, one at a time, and watch the panel fill.",
  "An octagon tile with a rotated square inside, in glaze colours.",
  "A game mechanic. It can feel childish if overdone and rewards speed over care, which is the wrong incentive for risk decisions.","M"),
 (20,"Marey's graphical timetable","Information graphics","Ibry, popularised by É.-J. Marey","https://chance.amstat.org/?p=6922",
  "Time on one axis and the route on the other: the slope of each line shows where each train is headed, how fast and where it pauses. An entire schedule can be understood in a glance; Tufte put it on the cover of his book.",
  "A time-space chart of the day. Stages run down the side, hours run across, and every order is a line. Steep means fast; a flat dotted line means it is stalled waiting for the customer. Four red lines have been flat for over two hours.",
  "You look for flat red lines and open those orders.",
  "Two crossing slanted lines, one red and one navy, forming an arch.",
  "It is superb for finding stalled orders and for seeing the pace of the day, and poor for reading what one order says. It needs the list for everything else.","M"),
]
MK={i:getattr(m_a,'m%d'%i,None) or getattr(m_b,'m%d'%i,None) or getattr(m_c,'m%d'%i) for i in range(1,21)}
FONTS="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Hanken+Grotesk:wght@400;600;700;800&family=Jost:wght@400;500;700&family=Barlow+Condensed:wght@500;700&family=Zen+Kaku+Gothic+New:wght@300;400;500&family=Source+Sans+3:wght@200;300;400;600&family=Libre+Baskerville:wght@400;700&family=Archivo:wght@400;600;700;800&family=Atkinson+Hyperlegible:wght@400;700&family=Silkscreen&family=Noto+Serif:wght@400;700&family=Noto+Naskh+Arabic:wght@500;700&family=Noto+Kufi+Arabic:wght@500;800&family=Public+Sans:wght@400;600;700;800&family=Noto+Sans:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400&display=swap"
def card(s):
    n,name,cat,who,url,belief,becomes,inter,logo,cost,eff=s
    return f'''<article class="cx" id="s{n}"><input type="checkbox" id="z{n}" class="zt"><header><span class="no">{n:02d}</span><div><h2>{esc(name)}</h2><p>{esc(cat)} · {esc(who)} · <a href="{url}">source</a></p></div><label for="z{n}" class="zb">Enlarge</label></header>
<div class="tx"><div><h4>What the school believes</h4><p>{esc(belief)}</p></div><div><h4>So the product becomes</h4><p>{esc(becomes)}</p></div></div>
<div class="frame"><div class="zoomer">{MK[n]()}</div></div>
<div class="ft"><div class="lg">{LG[n]}</div><p><b>You use it by:</b> {esc(inter)}</p><p><b>Logo:</b> {esc(logo)}</p><p><b>Honest cost</b> (build effort {eff}): {esc(cost)}</p></div></article>'''
jump=''.join(f'<a href="#s{s[0]}">{s[0]} {esc(s[1].split(",")[0].split(":")[0])}</a>' for s in S)
css=open('page.css').read()+open('mocks.css').read()
out=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Wasel: 20 schools</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link href="{FONTS}" rel="stylesheet"><style>{css}</style></head><body>
<header class="top"><h1>Wasel: 20 schools, 20 structures</h1><p class="lead">Each school was researched on its own (one search each, source linked on its card). The brief on each card is written from what the school actually believes, then turned into the structure of the product: a command sheet, a shelf, a storyboard, a ledger, a room that is silent unless something is wrong. All twenty use the same nine orders. Click Enlarge on a card to see it at full size.</p>
<p class="lead small">Three of them (Wabi-sabi, Hashiya, Hurufiyya) lean on Arabic and Tunisian material; none has been checked by a native reader. Several are not design schools in the strict sense (triage, accounting, library science, aviation): they are institutions whose philosophy of information is so clear it can drive a product. Each card says so.</p>
<nav class="jump">{jump}</nav></header><main class="pg"><div class="grid">{"".join(card(s) for s in S)}</div></main></body></html>'''
open('index.html','w').write(out); print('ok',len(out))
